{0 Frontend}

The Kairos frontend is split into two syntax levels.

{[
text file
  -> lexer / parser
  -> Kx_surface_syntax.source
  -> Kx_elaborate.elaborate_source
  -> Kx_ast.program
  -> Kairos_to_model
  -> Verification_model.node_model
]}

The verification pipeline consumes the elaborated AST, not the surface AST.
This keeps source conveniences out of the core engine.

{1 Surface Syntax}

[Kx_surface_syntax] is the AST produced directly by the parser.
It keeps all frontend conveniences explicit:

- enum declarations, which also provide the finite sets used for indexing;
- indexed variable families such as [signal[Route]];
- bounded [forall] / [exists] over enum types;
- local predicates;
- named actions;
- observers with explicit [init] and [step] blocks;
- history aliases;
- [history def] declarations and [history name(x)] applications;
- statement-level [for] loops.

This AST is useful for diagnostics and frontend tests, but it is not the AST
used by the verification engine.

{1 Elaboration}

[Kx_elaborate] lowers surface syntax to [Kx_ast].
The pass expands or removes the syntactic extensions:

- indexed variable families are expanded using enum constructors and become
  scalar variables with stable names;
- bounded quantifiers over enum types become finite conjunctions or
  disjunctions;
- local predicates are expanded at their call sites;
- named actions and [for] loops are inlined into ordinary statements;
- [history def] applications become generated scalar ghosts, generated ghost
  updates, and phase-specific transition [ensures] clauses declared by the
  corresponding definition;
- observers become generated scalar ghosts plus generated assignments on
  transitions.

There is no separate [domain] declaration in the source language. A declaration
such as [type Route = R1 | R2;] is both the value type [Route] and the finite
set used by [signal[Route]], [forall r in Route], and [for r in Route].

Elaboration does not add a compatibility layer. Unknown predicates are rejected
instead of being preserved as implicit uninterpreted predicates.

{1 Core Frontend AST}

[Kx_ast] is the small AST accepted by the rest of the Kairos
implementation. It contains ordinary declarations, scalar variables, ordinary
statements, invariants and LTL contracts. It no longer contains surface
constructs such as [SSFor], [SSActionCall], [SHForall] or
frontend predicate declarations. It also no longer contains observer
declarations.

{1 Historical Definitions}

The source language provides a generic way to define reusable historical
summaries for specifications:

{[
history def first_value(x: HExpr): int
  init = x;
  init ensures: self = pre(x);
  step = pre(self);
  step ensures: self = pre(self);
]}

A definition has one historical-expression parameter, a result type, an [init]
expression, a [step] expression, and optional phase-specific [ensures] clauses.
The special name [self] denotes the generated history value. [init] is injected
on transitions leaving the initial state; [step] is injected on the other
transitions. Update expressions are pure frontend expressions: [step] may read
[pre(self)] and bounded [pre_k(self,k)] with constant [k], while bare [self] is
rejected so the temporal offset is explicit.

The definition is used in formulas with [history name(x)]:

{[
ensures: X G(history first_value(x) >= 0);
]}

History definitions are not core syntax. The parser records applications as
[SHHistoryCall], and [Kx_elaborate] replaces each use by a generated ghost
whose name is reserved under the [__kairos_history_] prefix. The source
language rejects user identifiers starting with [__kairos_] so that users
cannot name these internals directly.

The optional [init ensures] and [step ensures] clauses are not LTL formulas and
do not create a new temporal logic. They elaborate to local transition
postconditions used as auxiliary proof facts. For input parameters, [pre(x)] in
such a clause denotes the input sample consumed by the transition; for [self],
[pre(self)] remains a real one-step historical reference to the generated ghost.

Generated history ghosts are proof-only state. Source guards, source
assignments and actions cannot depend on them except through the generated
[init] and [step] blocks. Public contracts may mention them only through
[history name(x)] applications; ordinary user ghosts and observers remain
forbidden in [requires] and [ensures].

This mechanism is generic: [first_value], running maxima, counters, sums, or
domain-specific summaries are source definitions, not names hardcoded in the
frontend.

High-level safety schemas such as [maintainedUntil] are not parser
constructors. They should be written as [spec def] declarations, for example
[spec def maintainedUntil(t: Formula, i: Formula, r: Formula) =
G($t -> X($i W $r));].

Some constructs are real core-language features rather than pure syntax sugar:

- enum types and enum literals;
- pure first-order functions with contracts;
- ghost variables.

Pure functions are non-historical. Their contracts and bodies cannot mention
[pre], [preK] or local predicates. They are intended to translate directly to
Why3 functions.

{1 Observers}

Observers are generated ghost state variables, not a fixed set of operators.
An observer declares its type and two statement blocks:

{[
observers
  total: int
    init {
      total := delivered;
    }
    step {
      total := total + delivered;
    }
]}

The [init] block is injected on transitions leaving the initial state. The
[step] block is injected on all other transitions. Each block must assign the
observer itself on every path, and may assign only that observer. The [init]
block cannot read any observer, and the [step] block may read only its own
observer value.

The generated variable is placed in [sem_ghosts], not [sem_locals]. Source
transition guards, transition bodies and actions cannot read observers, so an
observer cannot influence outputs, calls or control flow. Observers are also
recorded as public specification ghosts: public [requires] and [ensures]
contracts may mention them, while ordinary user ghosts remain private. This
keeps observers as scalar history summaries for public trace specifications and
prevents the observer section from becoming a second transition language.

In LTL and first-order formulas, a direct boolean call [p(x)] accepts identifier
arguments. For indexed references or general expressions, inject an executable
expression into the formula with braces, for example
[{ isIdleState(routeState[r]) } = true].

{1 HPred}

[HPred] is still present in the core logical expression type. It denotes an
explicit predicate application at the core level, and some lower layers still
know how to carry it.

The Kairos source frontend does not create [HPred] as a fallback for an
undeclared source predicate. A source predicate must be declared locally and is
expanded by [Kx_elaborate]; otherwise parsing/elaboration fails. This
prevents accidental proof obligations over names that the program never
defined.

{1 Debug Dumps}

The CLI exposes frontend boundary dumps:

- [--dump-surface=FILE] writes the parsed [Kx_surface_syntax.source];
- [--dump-elaborated=FILE] writes the elaborated source with [Kx_ast];
- [--dump-normalized-program=FILE] writes the normalized program after the
  later frontend/model pipeline.

Use these dumps to check whether a language extension is still surface syntax
or has reached the core engine.

{1 Main Modules}

- [Kx_parser] and [Kx_lexer]: source parser.
- [Kx_surface_syntax]: parser output AST.
- [Kx_elaborate]: surface-to-core elaboration.
- [Kx_ast]: elaborated frontend AST consumed by the pipeline.
- [Kx_parse_api]: public parsing API and JSON debug serialization.
- [Kairos_to_model]: lowering from frontend AST to the verification
  model.
